WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.880
The subject of hybrid mana has been pretty&nbsp;
front and center recently in EDH discourse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.760
and I honestly find it a bit tiresome as a&nbsp;
topic. So, this video won’t be about the merits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.720
of the proposed hybrid mana change, not exactly&nbsp;
anyway. If you want a video that covers the meat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.480
of the issue I’ll be linking a Maldhound&nbsp;
video on the subject that I quite liked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.080
but now that the dust has settled a bit and&nbsp;
the most sharp-edged of hybrid gladiators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.600
have dulled their roll a bit, I want to&nbsp;
talk about the conversation around it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.640
and what it means for us. I will only be&nbsp;
discussing the hybrid mana change insofar as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.120
is necessary to make a case that the stakes&nbsp;
here are low and that the reaction to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.640
suggestion has been quite a notable and anomalous&nbsp;
event, because regardless of whether the change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.240
itself ends up happening, this has clearly&nbsp;
been an event in the online EDH community.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.680
For any of you blissfully out of the&nbsp;
loop, a couple weeks back Gavin Verhey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.440
of Wizards of the Coast made a set of&nbsp;
announcements, both in livestream form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.600
and in post form. A lot of it was pretty&nbsp;
reasonable tweaks to the bracket system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.440
those being some clarification of the language&nbsp;
of the different brackets, and the moving of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.600
a handful of cards off of the gamechanger list.&nbsp;
The more controversial part of the announcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.840
was the potential future changes part, where&nbsp;
Gavin discussed the potential future banning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.960
of Rhystic Study and Thassa’s Oracle, and the&nbsp;
possible tweak of allowing hybrid symbols to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.600
considered either of their colors for the sake of&nbsp;
color identity. The conversation that has spawned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.000
forth about hybrid mana has kinda fascinated me&nbsp;
in the past couple weeks, mainly because of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.960
unexpectedly strong and unexpectedly plentiful&nbsp;
the opinions have been, particularly opinions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.680
from the anti-hybrid mana side. There was an&nbsp;
initial wave of anti-hybrid youtube videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.480
and all videos about the subject have seen massive&nbsp;
numbers of comments, with a lot of people making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.960
a heated case for their side in this debate.&nbsp;
This has been fascinating to me because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.360
hybrid mana change seems like such a low stakes&nbsp;
issue, no matter what side of it you fall on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.080
but the community response to the suggested&nbsp;
change has felt like what it would be if WotC was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.520
suggesting literally abolishing color identity.&nbsp;
So, I want to dig a bit into why that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.280
what caused this, and to start let’s talk about&nbsp;
literally abolishing color identity—kind of. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.400
an episode of the Magic Mirror podcast released&nbsp;
in March, I gave a sort of devil’s advocate case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.680
for why color identity should be abolished,&nbsp;
and here are a couple key points from that.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.840
1. Magic: the Gathering is a game based around&nbsp;
mana and colors, with card designs existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.600
around the fact that colors are malleable&nbsp;
and decks can venture between colors at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.000
cost of deck space and deck consistency.&nbsp;
There are a lot of designs that color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.880
identity renders clunky, awkward, and nonsensical,&nbsp;
and this restricts players who wish to play them.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.440
2. Advocates of color identity will argue&nbsp;
that limitations are good for decks, but color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.760
identity doesn’t achieve this in a way that is&nbsp;
consistent or fair. Somebody can play a five color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.480
commander and avoid this restriction entirely,&nbsp;
whereas somebody who enjoys a specific monocolor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.720
commander that wants extra support from other&nbsp;
colors gets unfairly limited. Commander is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.320
a casual format about expression where players&nbsp;
can always choose their own limitations if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.760
want them, and the format imposing limitations&nbsp;
on its own goes against this spirit a bit.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.360
I think these arguments are interesting, and&nbsp;
they make me curious what a world without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.160
color identity would look like. Despite this,&nbsp;
in that podcast I mentioned that I ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.480
don’t support the abolition of color identity at&nbsp;
this point, preferring instead a set of changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.360
that fix two cases where color identity feels&nbsp;
particularly unintuitive to me, those being cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.560
whose identity is defined by indicators rather&nbsp;
than symbols, and hybrid mana. This is more or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.720
less still the position I hold this position&nbsp;
eight months later—color identity should stay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.440
but fixing up these weird side cases would be neat&nbsp;
and probably good for the format in the long term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.680
You can agree with that or not, and I’m not&nbsp;
here to convince you about it because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.320
isn’t a video about hybrid mana. Given that, you&nbsp;
may be wondering why I’m even bringing this up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.000
and that’s because the comments on&nbsp;
that video basically agree with me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.640
and that’s interesting. In the context of&nbsp;
hearing a devil’s advocate argument against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.440
color identity and then being presented with&nbsp;
these softer changes, people went “oh yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.240
I like color identity but those changes make&nbsp;
sense.” Am I some kind of god of rhetoric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.000
fooling innocent commander players into supporting&nbsp;
a hybrid mana change by positioning it as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.600
reasonable centrist position in the color identity&nbsp;
debate? Maybe, but regardless of framing I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.160
to wonder what other elements contributed&nbsp;
to this shift. The timing? The atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.520
within the EDH community? The party making&nbsp;
the suggestion? I don’t know the exact split,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.200
but my best guess is that it’s probably a mix of&nbsp;
these things, and I’m going to go through these.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.720
Firstly, the timing. One version of this idea is&nbsp;
that players dislike this change happening amidst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.280
hybrid mana being more heavily printed, both&nbsp;
in recent sets and in the upcoming set Lorwyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.320
Eclipse. I don’t think this is a major factor,&nbsp;
or I guess I hope it isn’t, because it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.360
a very silly thing to be a major factor. Of course&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast is going to suggest changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.720
to the commander format at times where it feels&nbsp;
most salient based on what is currently being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.280
printed, in fact it’d be a lot weirder if they&nbsp;
made a hybrid mana change during a period where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.080
very little hybrid mana was getting printed.&nbsp;
Just so I don’t leave it unsaid, this is a minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.240
change and isn’t going to meaningfully increase&nbsp;
sales of Lorwyn Eclipse, and WotC knows that. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.800
the bigger factor here is what has happened in&nbsp;
the recent past. We just came out of a summer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.440
saw a lot of dissatisfaction from the community,&nbsp;
with the Spiderman set being both an aesthetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.400
jarring Universes Beyond set and a poor quality&nbsp;
set in its own right. This was paired with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.040
announcement that next year will feature four&nbsp;
different standard legal Universes Beyond sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.680
out of a dizzying total of seven sets. I don’t&nbsp;
think I have the same emotional reaction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.920
this as a lot of people, but it’s certainly a&nbsp;
bummer—I don’t like the Spiderman set, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.560
don’t like being overwhelmed with endless standard&nbsp;
legal Universes Beyond sets. All of this to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.560
there has been a lot to be frustrated about, and&nbsp;
the online magic community has been hotboxing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.480
anger and resentment in all of its various spaces&nbsp;
and subspaces and niches for the past several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.960
months. With the Spiderman set being past us,&nbsp;
that anger has been looking for a new target to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.240
explode out at, and, this month that something&nbsp;
ended up being a suggested hybrid mana change.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.240
This timing factor pairs with the Who of&nbsp;
the announcement. The fact that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.840
Wizards of the Coast making an announcement&nbsp;
suggesting this change rather than a handsome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.840
and big-brained youtuber talking about&nbsp;
it in a podcast definitely had an effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.920
because Wizards of the Coast is in a rough&nbsp;
spot with community trust right now. I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.400
know who exactly to pin the blame on for every&nbsp;
decision, but the collective decisionmaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.720
power at Wizards of the Coast and its parent&nbsp;
company Hasbro has been making a lot of pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.680
shortsighted choices, and choices that they&nbsp;
know will bother the playerbase. This is not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.480
say that it’s been all bad for EDH—the bracket&nbsp;
system rollout has gone pretty smoothly IMHO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.520
and the general quality of products aimed at the&nbsp;
Commander format has been pretty decent lately.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.160
But, that’s arguably part of the problem—the&nbsp;
commander format has been identified as a cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.360
cow, and a whole lot of decisions about&nbsp;
what gets printed, when it gets printed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.800
and how it gets printed seem to be motivated by&nbsp;
milking that cash cow. So, it’s in this context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.280
that suggestion of a decision that could be made&nbsp;
about the commander format garners a massive wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.760
of backlash, getting lumped in with all the other&nbsp;
decisions that have been made in recent years.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.160
So, is this a defense of the hybrid-hating&nbsp;
playerbase? On some level it absolutely is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.240
in the sense that I think much of the current&nbsp;
climate that produced this is directly due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:03.840
decisions made by WotC and/or Hasbro. But, are&nbsp;
people justified in being this angry about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.480
change? I would say no. A lot of the arguments&nbsp;
against hybrid mana are reheated versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.280
of the arguments defending color identity I’ve&nbsp;
heard—limitations are good, homogenization is bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.960
designer intent doesn’t matter, commander format&nbsp;
architect intentions do matter, and so on. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.200
arguments in favor of color identity, these are&nbsp;
certainly valid, though a mixed bag in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.880
how persuasive I find them personally. Dredged up&nbsp;
and put into this new context though, they look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.720
very strange because the stakes are so much lower.&nbsp;
It’s like rhetorical homeopathy—the angle you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.880
arguing is very minor, almost inconsequential,&nbsp;
and when you argue it with the passion of somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.120
whose mother just got insulted, it makes you&nbsp;
look like a goofball. Like take the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.440
“does hybrid mana weaken color identity?”&nbsp;
My answer would be only a tiny bit at most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.200
particularly if Wizards of the Coast is more&nbsp;
careful with the hybrid color pie in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.880
which I kinda assume they will be for reasons I’m&nbsp;
throwing on screen but will not pause to elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.320
on. Then that tiny bit needs to get sifted through&nbsp;
the secondary question of how much does color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.800
identity erosion harm the commander format. Even&nbsp;
if you think the erosion of color identity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.120
pretty harmful to Commander, which is a reasonable&nbsp;
thing to believe, you need to remember that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.120
are not talking about the abolition of color&nbsp;
identity, you’re talking about a niche change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.360
to a very specific set of cards that already have&nbsp;
valid arguments to exist within the current logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.920
of color identity. I think a lot of players forget&nbsp;
that and just pretend they’re arguing against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:27.760
abolishing of color identity, and the result is&nbsp;
that conversations end up feeling deeply strange.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.440
Or, take the idea that an expansion of hybrid&nbsp;
mana will allow for the printing of more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.960
widely available broken stuff in the future. The&nbsp;
video from Tomer over at MTGGoldfish is the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.120
articulation of this argument that I’ve seen, and&nbsp;
there is a small part of me that worries what’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.000
happen when we get a Captain America’s Wrath next&nbsp;
year in hybrid red white that’s broken and $120&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.320
and desired by every red or white deck. That could&nbsp;
happen, but to contextualize how problematic that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.280
is, let’s go through the steps of that argument.&nbsp;
First of all, how problematic is it if broadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:01.920
available, powerful cards are printed? Well, it’s&nbsp;
something that has more or less always happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.520
throughout Magic: the Gathering’s history, but we&nbsp;
can say that it’s less than ideal in the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.800
something like The One Ring that is colorless and&nbsp;
also hits other formats. Step two of this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.840
is how much does hybrid mana actually impact this?&nbsp;
And the answer is that I don’t see a whole lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.640
evidence that this change will lead to a surge in&nbsp;
that. Wizards of the Coast already has colorless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.680
cards that they can use to give a power card to&nbsp;
any commander deck, so the best tool for this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.960
already available. In addition, if we look at the&nbsp;
current hybrid mana cards affected by this change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.720
none are staples in their two color identities—the&nbsp;
top two are Cold-Eyed Selkie and Saheeli, Sublime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.680
Artificer, not exactly terrifying must-plays.&nbsp;
Will Wizards of the Coast start rubbing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.680
hands together evilly and send hybrid Stock Up&nbsp;
or Beast Whisperer to the printers the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.840
the hybrid change happens? Maybe, but probably&nbsp;
not. One again, this is rhetorical homeopathy—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.520
risks in this particular case just aren’t&nbsp;
that high, and players are using mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.520
stand-ins to make their arguments rather&nbsp;
than discussing the reality of the situation.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.040
To be clear, it’s entirely fair for people&nbsp;
to be against the hybrid mana change for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.200
either of these two reasons, the direction&nbsp;
of the disagreement makes sense to me. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.080
the scale of the disagreement looks&nbsp;
rather strange, which is why I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.160
other factors I laid out earlier are fueling&nbsp;
the heat of this discussion rather than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.640
change itself. I don’t think this change is very&nbsp;
high-stakes for the two reasons I just laid out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:23.600
and even as Wizards of the Coast has been making&nbsp;
decisions dislike, I also really don’t buy most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.680
versions of slippery slope arguments that claim&nbsp;
this is just the beginning, two little waves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:32.400
over the top of the conceptual dam that is color&nbsp;
identity. Phyrexian Mana isn’t going to get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.560
treatment because it’s already been identified&nbsp;
by both WotC and the community as a design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:40.720
outlier. Off-color ability cards aren’t going&nbsp;
to get added because it’d be simply too large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.600
a change and would require other format changes&nbsp;
to even remotely make sense. And, full-fledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.240
color identity abolition definitely isn’t going&nbsp;
to happen when even an abolition-curious player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.320
like myself knows it’s a bad idea. I also&nbsp;
think it’s important to remember that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:58.560
change wasn’t invented by Wizards of the Coast&nbsp;
two seconds ago. This is something that players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.040
and designers alike have suggested and made cases&nbsp;
for in the past, including myself several months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.760
ago. Not everything needs to be some nefarious&nbsp;
machination by a corporation, and to pretend that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:13.120
a hybrid mana change is a brand new thing asked&nbsp;
for by no players is just plainly dishonest.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:16.800
At the end of the day, I still understand why&nbsp;
people tar the decision makers at Wizards of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.200
the Coast as greedy and corporate and nefarious&nbsp;
and other such pejoratives. It seems like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.680
year has really been an inflection point around&nbsp;
Universes Beyond due to it suddenly making up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.160
a large portion of standard sets. The result is&nbsp;
that even many players who were relatively neutral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.000
about it in previous years such as myself&nbsp;
have started to sour on it pretty heavily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.440
and the people who were already against it&nbsp;
are finding themselves wading into deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.120
and deeper levels of dissatisfaction. The&nbsp;
current trajectory of the game is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:41.826
good one, and though I’d reject the doomerism&nbsp;
that communities of internet-enjoyers tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.826 --> 00:11:46.160
to cultivate, there is reason to be unhappy.&nbsp;
That said, I also hope we as a playerbase can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:50.080
get better at calibrating the temperature&nbsp;
of our discussions. When we respond with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.800
disproportionate levels of anger and gloom to&nbsp;
lower-stakes suggestions, I worry that it weakens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.360
our ability to be taken seriously on the things&nbsp;
that matter more, and also distracts from those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:03.680
more important things. There are real decisions&nbsp;
being made that have the potential to seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.400
impact the future trajectory of the game, and&nbsp;
the impacts of the hybrid mana suggestion are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:13.520
microscopic compared to the addition of even a&nbsp;
single extra standard-legal Universes Beyond set.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.880
I also think it’s better for the health of&nbsp;
the community to not blow every announcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.880
and update way out of proportion. When I see&nbsp;
comments declaring every tiny thing as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.640
death of the game, it makes me think of the vast&nbsp;
array of spaces across the internet where people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:29.280
marinate in collective despair and pull themselves&nbsp;
deeper and deeper into conspiracy and doomerism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.440
It’s not that much of a problem just yet, but&nbsp;
that’s just something I’ve been getting little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.080
whiffs of this year, and it’s not healthy. Maybe&nbsp;
it’s because I’ve been involved in a wide range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:43.120
of game communities, from flourishing to actively&nbsp;
dying, but my general reaction to a game company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.760
doing stuff I dislike is to either distance&nbsp;
myself from it, or to take some control into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.400
my own hands. The first is absolutely helpful&nbsp;
sometimes, and I fully believe there are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:56.240
discontentedly sticking around with Magic&nbsp;
who probably need to move on to other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.680
but I’m going to focus on the second. If&nbsp;
what Wizards of the Coast is handing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:04.320
doesn’t satisfy your needs, build something&nbsp;
for yourself—a format, a subformat, a cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.040
a community, a different game, or just a playgroup&nbsp;
of people looking for the same sort of fun as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:13.680
are. The best antidote to feeling disempowered&nbsp;
and dissatisfied by the decisions of others is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:18.080
to take back control of your own enjoyment.&nbsp;
That’s undoubtedly harder than just accepting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:23.120
what a game company has to offer you, but the&nbsp;
potential benefits are well-worth the effort.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.480
Thanks for watching! And thanks to&nbsp;
Archidekt for supporting the channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.320
I’ve been working to finish my fall supporter&nbsp;
deck review, and it makes life so much easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:34.560
when I can port somebody’s deck into Archidekt,&nbsp;
do all the necessary categorization and analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.720
to get an idea of what’s going on, and then&nbsp;
have the in-website categories look nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.800
enough for me to be happy just throwing them&nbsp;
in a video as a visual. I’ve also been brewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:46.720
a couple new decks lately, and if you want to&nbsp;
check those out, a link to my decks is in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.120
description. I also want to thank my patrons&nbsp;
for their ongoing support of what I do here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:55.440
I had some stuff to say about the nature of&nbsp;
“design intent” that didn’t feel quite at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.400
home in this video, so I threw them in their&nbsp;
own separate video for supporters. If that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:04.960
something you want to hear me talk about, you&nbsp;
can become a YouTube member or Patron today.

